-- string-zero/stderr --
-- zero-type-params/divergence --
suite: emit-probes
case: zero-type-params.tck
go: Go's zeroValue and resultZero compare the type parameter itself with
  string, error and Allocator, so they write 0 as the zero value of a T
  standing for one, in a defer's result variable and in a checked pop,
  remove or delete, which C cannot convert to their structs: the C does
  not compile.
why: The port writes a defer's result string zeroed in full and error
  and Allocator values zeroed, the C Go writes in the same function
  written for the type T stands for, and a checked form's string as the
  pooled empty string, which Go's C for that function gets wrong too
  when nothing else pools it (see string-zero), under the lead's ruling
  of 2026-11-15 that the language already defines the zero values, which
  the language owner may overrule. The C compiles and runs under the
  address, undefined behavior and leak sanitizers with clang -Wall
  -Werror -Wno-unused-variable: Go's drop of a T that owns nothing
  leaves its const local unused, as Go writes it.
same: none
-- zero-type-params/exit --
0
